WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.801
-It's great to see you, buddy.
-It's lovely to see you.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.267
-In person. It's not on Zoom.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:06.767
-Yes.
-Yeah, do you like the Zoom?

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:08.634
-Although, Ariana looked
really beautiful on Zoom.

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.067
-Yes. She's just gorgeous, yeah.
-She was amazing.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.534
-Are you Zooming?

00:00:12.534 --> 00:00:15.267
-I've been Zooming a lot.
I guess it's helped us.

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:17.300
It's helped us through
this whole crazy time.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.367
-It's been wild.
-I have a few things coming out.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.934
I've been doing some press
recently on Zoom.

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:27.367
And it's funny because when you
are Zooming from home, you know,

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:29.567
some of your home life
gets in there.

00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:31.634
And I was working from
my girlfriend's place,

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:35.801
and she has two lovely children,
and her 8-year-old son

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:39.968
kind of snuck in the room
at the end of my Zoom interview.

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:43.467
And the journalist was saying,
"Well, it's -- you know,

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:47.734
you've been working for a long
time and you're the youngest

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:50.200
Academy Award winner to win

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.367
an Academy Award
for 'The Pianist.'"

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.834
And I see him start
jumping around.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:56.834
And I say, "Yes, thank you."

00:00:56.834 --> 00:00:59.067
And then he's freaking out
in the corner of the room.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:01.000
And then we wrapped up
the interview and he goes,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.734
"Your penis?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:05.567
"Your penis? You won an
Academy Award for your penis?!"

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.467
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.334
I said,
"No, that wasn't a category."

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.567
-"I'm just happy
to be nominated." Yeah.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:15.868
-It is the youngest.

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:17.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.868
-That's right. You actually won.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.200
That's hilarious.
How old are you? Twenty--

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.100
-Now? I was 29.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.901
-29 to win an Academy -- you're
the -- and I think you still are

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:27.968
the youngest actor
to win Best Actor.

00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:29.968
Congratulations on that.
-Yeah, thank you.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:32.400
Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.133
-When did you know you wanted to
get into acting?

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:36.267
Were you a teenager?

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.534
-I mean, I love acting.

00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:41.601
And I guess I started acting
really young.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.501
I think before acting, I had --

00:01:43.501 --> 00:01:46.467
I really was infatuated with
magic.

00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:49.234
-Yeah, me too.
-Yeah?

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:51.767
Did you stud--
Like, I studied magic and I --

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.734
-I used to check out books
at the library. Magic books.

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:59.701
-Yeah. Magic books and I'd learn
routines and I'd go to Tannen's.

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:03.000
There's a great magic store in
New York that I used to go to.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Try and con them to sell me
flash paper, which is, like,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.200
super flammable
and burn your house down.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.501
You know?
But I started at six years old.

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:11.868
I was, like, infatuated with
magic.

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:15.501
And by the time I was 11,
I was doing

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.501
children's birthday parties
who were a little younger,

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:20.467
and I was the "Amazing Adrien."

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:21.934
-Wow!
[ Cheers ]

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:24.501
Wow!
-For 50 bucks, I could have done

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:27.067
the whole party and --
-Started working young.

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.501
-Was working. Yeah.
-That's fantastic.

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:30.701
Do you remember
any of the tricks?

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:33.868
-Yeah. I mean, I haven't done
much in a long time, but yeah.

00:02:33.868 --> 00:02:35.000
-Could you do a trick?

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.601
-You want me to do
something like --

00:02:36.601 --> 00:02:37.701
-Could you do a trick now?

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.100
-I could do, like,
a party trick.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.601
This was, like,
when I was six though.

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:45.767
-Okay. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:48.834
-It's not quite great.
Are you game?

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:52.501
I need some tissues
or something,

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:54.000
just in case you or I
start crying.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:57.300
-I have tissues.
-Good. Alright.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.367
-Do you want the whole box?
-I'll do it.

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:00.767
No, tissues are cool.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:01.934
-Are we doing the trick?

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:03.100
-Couple boxes.
Yeah, we're doing the trick.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.367
We're doing the trick.
-This is fantastic.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:05.501
Alright, I just want to make
sure I'm doing it right.

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.067
-So you just settle down
right here, alright?

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:08.534
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:11.801
So, really, we'll get
the whole box of tissues.

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:14.067
So...
♪♪

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:15.767
Magic music.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.067
-Yeah, exactly.
-I like that, yeah.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.734
-You never thought you'd get
magic music, but yeah.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:22.033
-So, basically, you know, so
much of it is about, you know,

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:23.701
keeping your eye on
what you are doing.

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:26.133
So if I put this is in this hand
or this hand,

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:27.133
which hand would it be in?

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:28.667
-Be in this hand.

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:30.868
-Right. Or this one.

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:32.767
I'll show you again.
-Wow! Wait, wait, wait.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:34.400
[ Laughter and applause ]
-Very simple.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.334
We can stop. We can one-up it.
Now we have three of them.

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:40.300
It's hard sometimes because you
have to put it in there first,

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.367
but if you put it in this --
This one or this one?

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:46.334
-This one.

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:48.300
-Oh.
-Yeah.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.767
What is going on?!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:53.000
Wait, do I, like, have a brain
of a 6-year-old?

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:53.968
Is that what you're doing
right now?

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.434
-No, I just have it in there.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:56.834
-Alright, yeah, you got the
thing. You put it in your hand.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.033
-But everybody has to keep
their eyes on it, yeah.

00:03:58.033 --> 00:03:59.901
-Everyone can --
-I put it there.

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:01.601
This one or that one?

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:03.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.901
-I'm going to say --

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:06.234
-That one.
-Okay, wow.

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:08.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:12.834
♪♪

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.667
-Ah, I still got it.

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:16.734
-That's not bad. Not too shabby.
That was fantastic.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.400
-That was pretty good.
-Thank you for doing that.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.467
-It's good at a party
when everyone is really wasted.

00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:22.234
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.400
And then you went from
performing there

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.667
to becoming an actor.
You got the bug.

00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:28.167
I was going to say you're great
in all your films,

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.300
but I really love --

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.634
you and Wes Anderson
work fantastic together.

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:34.200
This is your fourth film
with Wes.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.868
I think you just did your fifth
film with Wes Anderson.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.434
You guys just have a thing
and you're fantastic in it.

00:04:40.434 --> 00:04:41.934
-Yeah, I'm really grateful
for it. Thank you.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:46.334
And he's just such
a wonderful -- he's a genius.

00:04:46.334 --> 00:04:48.667
And "The French Dispatch,"
which is out now,

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:51.934
is just such an amazing movie.

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:53.767
It's a privilege working
with him.

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:56.434
-Do you have any memories of
Wes where you go like...

00:04:56.434 --> 00:04:59.267
-I mean, I have so many, like,
life experiences with Wes.

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:01.334
We shot a movie.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:02.634
My first film that
I worked with him

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:04.567
was "The Darjeeling Unlimited,"
and we were India.

00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:05.567
-Yeah, of course.

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:08.234
-And I got to be brothers with

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:10.367
Jason Schwartzman
and Owen Wilson.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:12.801
-Fantastic.
-And we shot on a moving train,

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:15.367
and it was really
an amazing time.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.734
And I bought a Royal Enfield
motorcycle,

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:19.434
which is a really cool,
old British bike

00:05:19.434 --> 00:05:22.000
that's built in India,
and I was planning to ship it

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.033
home after shooting there.
-Yeah.

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:27.400
-And one day I was riding
and we were on the highway.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.868
I'm riding and there were
all these tuk-tuks,

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:33.567
these auto-rickshaws.

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:36.667
They're like carts, but they
were -- Those were the taxis.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:37.934
-Yes.
-They're little carts.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.234
And they were on a highway.

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:40.734
I'm jamming behind this guy.

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:44.467
And he veers left at full speed,
and right in front of him

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:49.067
is a cow walking away from me
on the highway.

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:54.634
And so I jam on the brakes
and I'm literally skidding,

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:58.501
about to rear-end the rear end
of a cow with my face.

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.467
[ Laughter ]
-Wow!

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:04.734
-And I was laughing as -- I was
laughing because I was like,

00:06:04.734 --> 00:06:06.601
"This is how they're all
going to remember me."

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:08.100
[ Laughter ]
I really -- I had --

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.234
It was like a near-death
experience,

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:10.234
and I was like
[ Imitates brakes ]

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:12.667
There it was. There it was.

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:14.200
It was a --
-There it was.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.868
-There it was. Sorry.

00:06:15.868 --> 00:06:17.534
-There -- There it was.

00:06:17.534 --> 00:06:18.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:19.834
Wow.
-He's waiting to find it.

00:06:19.834 --> 00:06:22.501
-Wow! Wow!

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:23.734
-I had to do it. Sorry.

00:06:23.734 --> 00:06:27.701
-Wow. There it was. Oh, my God.

00:06:27.701 --> 00:06:29.400
Wow. Okay. I get it.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.834
-You know what's crazy?

00:06:30.834 --> 00:06:33.734
It's a little tangent, but,
like, I was just talking with

00:06:33.734 --> 00:06:34.968
your producer and I was saying,
"Yeah, you know?"

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:36.200
They were like,
"Oh, you used to do magic."

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.767
I said, "Yeah, I'd love to do --

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:39.133
I'd love to, you know,
do a trick."

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:40.767
And he said, "Well, maybe you
can do one with Jimmy."

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.534
And I was visiting my mom.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:44.734
And my mom overheard just part
of the conversation.

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:49.000
We got off the phone, and she
goes, "So it's great.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.834
You're going to do a -- You're
going to do Jimmy's party?"

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:53.200
And she thought somehow now,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.467
someone was going to pay me
to do a birthday party.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:57.167
And I was on the phone
with a parent,

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:59.968
and the rest of the discussion
was more like peripheral,

00:06:59.968 --> 00:07:02.901
working a lot with
Wes Anderson.

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:06.067
-Yeah, then you're like, "Yeah,
but I'm also not giving --

00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:08.868
I'm not giving, but I'm gonna
play this little boy's party."

00:07:08.868 --> 00:07:10.467
[ Laughter ]
-It is your birthday.

00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:12.434
Every night is your birthday.
-You're the best for doing that.

00:07:12.434 --> 00:07:13.834
Thank you. I appreciate that.

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:15.701
How do you --
[ Applause ]

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.000
That was actually great.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.400
How do you describe
"The French Dispatch"?

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.501
And who do you play?

00:07:20.501 --> 00:07:22.667
-Yeah, it's a -- It's so fun.

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:27.467
It really is several vignettes,
several stories that are

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:30.067
through the eyes of
the journalists working at

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:33.767
"The French Dispatch,"
which is an American publication

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:35.267
that's based in France.

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:39.634
And it's wonderful
and all these stories.

00:07:39.634 --> 00:07:45.567
Our story is with Benicio
Del Toro and Lea Seydoux.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:48.501
And he plays an artist,
and I meet him in prison.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:55.534
And I become a pretty remarkable
art dealer, and I sell his work.

00:07:55.534 --> 00:07:58.400
-Yes, exactly right.
I want to show everybody a clip.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.734
It's also, by the way, just
a beautiful film as well.

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:02.467
-Isn't it beautiful? Yeah.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:04.667
Here's Adrien Brody
in "The French Dispatch."

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:05.734
Take a look at this.

00:08:05.734 --> 00:08:08.334
-We're done with flowers
and footballs.

00:08:08.334 --> 00:08:11.133
We're finished with beaches
and seascapes.

00:08:11.133 --> 00:08:14.400
We're getting out of armored
rugs and tapestries too.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.234
I found something new.

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:28.000
-Modern art?

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.534
-Modern art, our specialty
starting now.

00:08:30.534 --> 00:08:32.100
-I don't get it.
-Of course you don't.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.334
-Am I too old?
-Of course you are.

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:34.834
-Why is this good?
-It isn't good.

00:08:34.834 --> 00:08:36.801
Wrong idea.
-That's no answer.

00:08:36.801 --> 00:08:38.868
-My point.
You see the girl in it?

00:08:38.868 --> 00:08:41.767
-No.
-Trust me. She's there.

00:08:41.767 --> 00:08:43.334
-That's right.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:44.868
Adrien Brody, everybody.

00:08:44.868 --> 00:08:47.300
"The French Dispatch"
is in theaters now.

